{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792334801,"nonce":"h3EhbSDBUSPasQ7Bs/+iYSi8Pvk=","message":"Callees first: before a body is optimised, each small program callee outside its call cycle is optimised, through the same lazy-optimiser entry, so the caller inlines the callee as it runs and not as lowered, whichever was called first. Restricting it to callees outside the caller's cycle keeps every wait on a cell pointing down the call graph. `ZYNTAX_DISABLE_CALLEES_FIRST=1` turns it off.\n\nrichards was the clearest case: with HandlerTaskRec$deviceInAdd$trusted and workInAdd$trusted optimised first they are 25 instructions and call-free, and HandlerTask$fn inlines them; lowered they are 13 instructions with a call and are refused outside a loop. Inlining lowered bodies always (tried) was the slow arrangement.\n\nNUC, 4 idle pinned cores, CI's figure, 9 interleaved reps, on against off, zypy on 5c58f753: richards 0.726, crypto 0.802, telco 0.878, deltablue 0.892, raytrace 0.930, fft 0.960, float 0.966, meteor 0.968; nqueens 1.024 at 21 reps (its hot functions' inline decisions are the same either way); the rest within noise. zylua, 9 reps: op_overload 0.896, method_call 0.950, collatz 0.961, records 0.964, lambda_call and closure_call noisy either way, the rest within noise. richards' steady state still comes out slow in about one run in eight.","files":null}]}